title: Check a ticket's health whenever a command touches it, and warn on drift
created: 2026-10-08T01:19:58Z
updated: 2026-10-08T01:19:58Z
effort: M
area: cli
handoff:

Drift is only found when someone runs `pidly check`, and only edit validates
the ticket it writes (info_faults, bin/pidly:969). claim, release, set,
needs, close, reopen, reparent and reap all read and rewrite an info file
without looking at it, so a hand-edit that broke a ticket, a field the
schema has since tightened, or a needs: naming nothing is carried along
silently until the next full check — usually by someone else.

Every write command already has the ticket open under the lock. Run
info_faults on that one ticket (and the structural checks that concern it:
a claim under c/, an open child under a closed parent, a needs: nothing
resolves) after the write, and print what it finds to stderr as warnings.

Constraints:

- Warn, never refuse. The command did what it was asked; a drifted ticket
  must stay claimable and closable, or the check becomes the thing that
  blocks work. Exit status unchanged.
- One ticket, not the tree. The cost has to stay negligible next to the
  command itself; the whole-tracker scan stays in check.
- Same wording as check, from the same code, so a warning can be looked up
  and the two never disagree. Notes (unknown field, missing slot) stay
  notes, or a ticket written by hand nags on every touch.
- Read-only commands (list, show, tree) stay quiet — a listing full of
  warnings is noise. Possibly show, since it is looking at one ticket
  anyway; decide when building it.
- A way to silence it (PIDLY_QUIET or similar) for scripts and the tests.

Open question: whether to check the ticket before the write too, so a
warning can say "was already wrong" versus "this command made it wrong" —
the second would be a pidly bug and worth saying loudly.

Related: 69 (claim left on a closed ticket) is one of the drifts this would
have surfaced at the moment close ran.
